+ beginning of the string, and \(L\) the length of the string, then the general form of \(y\), as in equation (1b) of App. III., p. 575b, is @@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===
\[\begin{aligned} y = {} & \sum_{n = 1}^{n = \infty} \left\{C_n \cdot \sin \frac{n\pi x}{L} \cdot \sin \frac{2\pi n}{T} \left(t - \frac{\tau}{2}\right)\right\} \\ @@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===
& + \sum_{n = 1}^{n = \infty} \left\{D_n \cdot \sin \frac{2\pi x}{L} \cdot \cos \frac{2\pi n}{T} \left(t - \frac{\tau}{2}\right)\right\} \end{aligned} \tag{3}\]@@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===


By comparing equations (2) and (3) we find immediately that all D's vanish, or \[D_n = 0,\] and @@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===
\[C_n \cdot \sin \frac{n\pi x}{L} = \frac{g + f}{\pi^2} \cdot \frac{T}{n^2} \cdot \sin \frac{n\pi\tau}{T} \tag{3a}\]@@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===


¶ Here \(g + f\) and \(\tau\) are independent of \(x\), but not of \(n\). On taking the equations for \(n = 1\) and \(n = 2\), and then dividing one by the other, there results @@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===
\[\frac{C_2}{C_1} \cdot \cos \frac{\pi x}{L} = \frac{1}{4} \cdot \cos \frac{\pi\tau}{T}.\]@@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===


From which it follows that for \(x = \frac{L}{2}\), \(\tau\) is also \(= \frac{T}{2}\), as observation shews. But if \(x = 0\), then according to observations \(\tau\) is also \(= 0\). Hence @@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===
\[C_2 = \frac{1}{4} \cdot C_1, \quad \text{and} \quad \frac{x}{L} = \frac{\tau}{T} \tag{3b}\] @@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===
so that \(g + f\) is independent of \(x\). Let \(v\) be the amplitude of the vibration of the point \(x\) in the string, then ¶ \[f\tau = g (T - \tau) = 2v,\] @@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===
\[g + f = \frac{2v}{\tau} + \frac{2v}{T - \tau} = \frac{2vT}{\tau (T - \tau)} = \frac{2vL^2}{Tx (L - x)}.\]@@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===


And since \(g + f\) is independent of \(x\), we must have \[v = 4V \cdot \frac{x (L - x)}{L^2}\] @@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===
where \(V\) is the amplitude in the middle of the string. From equation (3b) it follows that the sections \(\alpha\beta\) and \(\beta\gamma\) of the vibrational figure, fig. 62, p. 385b, must be proportional to the corresponding parts @@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===
of the string on both sides of the observed point. Hence we have finally @@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===
¶ \[y = \frac{8V}{\pi^2} \cdot \sum_{n = 1}^{n = \infty} \left\{\frac{1}{n^2} \cdot \sin \frac{n\pi x}{L} \cdot \sin \frac{2\pi n}{T} \left(t - \frac{\tau}{2}\right)\right\} \tag{3c}\] @@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===
for the complete expression of the motion of the string.@@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===


If we put \(t - \frac{\tau}{2} = 0\), \(y\) will = 0 for all values of \(x\), and hence all parts of the string pass through their position of rest simultaneously. From that time the velocity \(f\) of the point \(x\) is @@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===
\[f = \frac{2v}{\tau} = \frac{8V (L - x)}{LT}.\]@@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===


But this velocity lasts only during the time \(\tau\), where \(\tau = \frac{Tx}{L}\). Hence after the time \(t\), and @@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===
\[\text{as long as } t < \frac{Tx}{L}, \text{ we have } y = ft = \frac{8V}{LT} \cdot (L - x)\, t \tag{4}\] @@A@@
>>>
<<<
@@A@@
===
\[\text{and hence } y < \frac{8V}{L^2} \cdot x (L - x).\]
>>>
